<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qi Jiguang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1528">1528</a>–<a href="/years/1588">1588</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qi Jiguang was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> general who defeated the wokou <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> raiding the coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> in the 1550s and 1560s. He trained disciplined volunteer armies in small-unit tactics such as the 'mandarin duck' formation and wrote military manuals, including the Jixiao Xinshu. He later strengthened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a> defences near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qi-jiguang.jpg" alt="Qi Jiguang" width="320" height="593" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Chen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qi_Jiguang_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Notable campaigns included the Yongle Emperor's five expeditions into Mongolia, the occupation of Vietnam (1407–1427), the suppression of coastal pirates (wokou) by commanders such as <b>Qi Jiguang</b>, and the intervention in Korea against the Japanese invasions of 1592–1598.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Up to 25,000 watchtowers are estimated to have been built, and under general <b>Qi Jiguang</b> 1,200 were raised between Shanhaiguan Pass and Changping in 1567–1570, while sections of the rammed-earth wall were faced with brick.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall of China · How It Was Made</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.4,118.4&amp;year=1567" aria-label="Shandong on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7528 851 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Qi Jiguang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7978 1121h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7978 1121h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.4,118.4&amp;year=1567">What was here in 1567? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
